  "estimatedMinutes": 1,
  "statement": "Let \\(m\\) and \\(n\\) be any two odd numbers, with \\(n\\) less than \\(m.\\) The largest integer which divides all possible numbers of the form \\(m^2-n^2\\) is:",
  "answerChoices": [
    {
      "label": "A",
      "text": "\\(2\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "B",
      "text": "\\(4\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "C",
      "text": "\\(6\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "D",
      "text": "\\(8\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "E",
      "text": "\\(16\\)"
    }
  ],
  "answer": {
    "letter": "d",
    "value": "8"
  },
  "solutions": [
    {
      "id": "sol.ahsme-1960-p25.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "We have \\[ m^2-n^2=(m-n)(m+n). \\] Both factors are even, and one is divisible by \\(4,\\) so every such difference is divisible by \\(8.\\) Taking \\(m=3,n=1\\) gives \\(m^2-n^2=8,\\) proving that no larger integer always divides it.\n\nThus, the correct answer is D.",
      "hints": [
        "Factor \\(m^2-n^2\\) into two even factors",
        "Among \\(m-n\\) and \\(m+n,\\) one is divisible by \\(4\\); then test a small pair"
      ],
      "images": []
    }
  ],
